`check-config` refuses exactly what `run` would refuse, and exits 2 for it —
the same code as clap's usage errors, because a config this tool cannot act
on is your input, not the bus's answer. Every problem is named by its path
into the document.

A config that checks clean says what it holds:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/valid.json5
tests/cmd/fixtures/valid.json5: 4 rule(s), 4 sink(s), doctor every 6h, published as ops-1/zenwatch — ok

```

An unknown field is a rule that silently pages nobody, refused at parse:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/unknown-field.json5
? 2
Error: tests/cmd/fixtures/unknown-field.json5: unknown field `methd`, expected one of `url`, `method`, `headers`, `timeout_s` at line 3 column 10

```

Secrets are never inline. A bare string in a secret position does not parse —
the point is that a config that can be checked in cannot carry a credential
by accident:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/inline-secret.json5
? 2
Error: tests/cmd/fixtures/inline-secret.json5: secrets are never inline — write { env: "NAME" } or { file: "/path" } at line 3 column 10

```

A sink a rule names must exist:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/missing-sink.json5
? 2
  rules[0].sinks[0]: "pager" is not in `sinks` (which has: hook)
Error: tests/cmd/fixtures/missing-sink.json5: 1 problem(s) — nothing was run

```

The vocabulary is closed — no expressions, no templating — and the refusal
spells all eleven kinds:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/bad-rule.json5
? 2
  rules[0].rule: "if rate(zk2/**) > 5 then page": is not a rule — the vocabulary is closed (no expressions, no templating): rate-above <SEL> <HZ> | rate-below <SEL> <HZ> | silent-for <SEL> <SECS> | invalid-payload <SEL> | qos-mismatch <SEL> | doctor <CHECK-ID> | alert-firing <SEL> [info|warning|critical] | dropped | instance-gone <SYSTEM/SERVICE> | liveliness-gone <SEL> | alerts <SEL>
Error: tests/cmd/fixtures/bad-rule.json5: 1 problem(s) — nothing was run

```

The alarm rules read `alarms.v1` (#800, AD): `alerts`, one state per alarm
key, and `alert-firing`, one state per rule at its floor. Their selectors are
base-relative, read through the session in the deployment's namespace:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/alarms.json5
tests/cmd/fixtures/alarms.json5: 2 rule(s), 1 sink(s), no service (publish: false) — ok

```

— so an alarm selector written with the namespace on it, as a window rule's
wire selector is, is refused, saying where it is read:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/alarms-namespaced.json5
? 2
  rules[0].rule: alerts "site/zk2/*/*/alarms.v1/state/alarms/*" is not base-relative: an alarm selector is read through the session in the deployment's namespace (bus.namespace, --namespace), so it starts at zk2/ — unlike a window rule's wire selector, which is watched in no namespace and carries it; write "zk2/*/*/alarms.v1/state/alarms/*"
Error: tests/cmd/fixtures/alarms-namespaced.json5: 1 problem(s) — nothing was run

```

Two rules on one engine condition could not be told apart by the transition's
`rule` string, so the second is refused:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/duplicate-condition.json5
? 2
  rules[1].rule: duplicates rules[0]'s condition "silent-for site/zk2/*/*/*/stream/** 30" — the engine reports one transition per condition; give the second rule a different condition or merge the sinks
Error: tests/cmd/fixtures/duplicate-condition.json5: 1 problem(s) — nothing was run

```

A secret is resolved at check time: an unset variable is a config problem now,
not a delivery failure at 3am:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/unset-env.json5
? 2
  sinks.ops.token: environment variable ZENWATCH_UNSET_TOKEN is not set
Error: tests/cmd/fixtures/unset-env.json5: 1 problem(s) — nothing was run

```

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/bad-tick.json5
? 2
  tick_s: must be a positive number of seconds, got 0
Error: tests/cmd/fixtures/bad-tick.json5: 1 problem(s) — nothing was run

```

A file that is not there:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/nope.json5
? 2
Error: cannot read tests/cmd/fixtures/nope.json5: No such file or directory (os error 2)

```

## What the zk2 port took away (#614, FL2)

Each refused by name, with where its question went — never an unknown-field
error that says nothing, and never parsed into a rule that silently never
fires: v1's `origin-down` (now `instance-gone`, no alias), `alerts` over v1's
alert plane (what to write instead: alarms.v1's keys, #800 AD), inhibition
switched on (removed: it read v1's catalog), grouping by v1's origin chunk,
v1's registry dirs, and the doctor's v1 sample:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/zk2-refusals.json5
? 2
  bus.registry: v1's registry slices are gone: zk2 reads contracts — `contracts: [<PATH>]` (an authoring file, a directory of them, or a .history root) or `--contracts`; a revision held offline is never retrieved, every other one comes from the bus (spec §8.4)
  discipline.group_by[0]: "origin" was v1's origin chunk, which zk2 keys do not carry: group by `system` (the <system> of <system>/<service>, a host under hostid.v1) or `address` (<system>/<service>)
  discipline.inhibit: inhibition was removed in the zk2 port (#614): it read v1's catalog (edge/*, entity/*, alias/*) to attribute a symptom to a down host, and no zk2 profile carries those documents. Delete the block, or set `enabled: false`
  doctor.sample: v1's per-family state sample: zk2's deep checks (`state-stamp-foreign`, `population-over-bound`) read every owner's state once and take no sample — remove it
  rules[0].rule: "origin-down h-3fa9c2d41b7e": v1's origin-down read v1's liveliness roster; in zk2 it is instance-gone <SYSTEM/SERVICE>: an address with no instance token visible to this reader (spec §8.1) — the vocabulary is closed (no expressions, no templating): rate-above <SEL> <HZ> | rate-below <SEL> <HZ> | silent-for <SEL> <SECS> | invalid-payload <SEL> | qos-mismatch <SEL> | doctor <CHECK-ID> | alert-firing <SEL> [info|warning|critical] | dropped | instance-gone <SYSTEM/SERVICE> | liveliness-gone <SEL> | alerts <SEL>
  rules[1].rule: "alerts v1/*/state/*/alert/*": v1's alert plane (`v1/<origin>/state/<producer>/alert/<alert_key>`) is gone: zk2's alarms are alarms.v1's, one key per alarm at zk2/<system>/<service>/alarms.v1/state/alarms/<alert_key>, read in the deployment's namespace. Write `zk2/*/*/alarms.v1/state/alarms/*`, or narrower (`zk2/<system>/*/alarms.v1/state/alarms/*`)
Error: tests/cmd/fixtures/zk2-refusals.json5: 6 problem(s) — nothing was run

```

`run` refuses the same, before any session opens — `check-config` and `run`
pass one gate:

```console
$ zenwatch run --config tests/cmd/fixtures/alerts.json5 --once --dry-run
? 2
  rules[0].rule: "alerts v1/*/state/*/alert/*": v1's alert plane (`v1/<origin>/state/<producer>/alert/<alert_key>`) is gone: zk2's alarms are alarms.v1's, one key per alarm at zk2/<system>/<service>/alarms.v1/state/alarms/<alert_key>, read in the deployment's namespace. Write `zk2/*/*/alarms.v1/state/alarms/*`, or narrower (`zk2/<system>/*/alarms.v1/state/alarms/*`)
Error: tests/cmd/fixtures/alerts.json5: 1 problem(s) — nothing was run

```

A state file v1 zenwatch wrote is refused, never migrated: its identities
name v1 origins and producers no zk2 rule produces. Moving it aside is the
fix, and what it costs — one re-announcement of what is still firing — is
then the operator's choice:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/state-v1.json5
? 2
  state_file: state file tests/cmd/fixtures/state-v1.json is version 1, written by zenwatch before the zk2 port: its identities name v1 origins, producers and alert refs, which no zk2 rule produces, so it is not migrated. Move it aside (or delete it) to start a fresh ledger — the first run then announces once what is still firing
Error: tests/cmd/fixtures/state-v1.json5: 1 problem(s) — nothing was run

```

The deployment flags decide refusals too, so `check-config` takes them: a
namespace that is not a concrete prefix, contracts that are not there —

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/valid.json5 --namespace prod/*
? 2
  --namespace: namespace "prod/*": a namespace is a concrete prefix and cannot hold a wildcard
Error: tests/cmd/fixtures/valid.json5: 1 problem(s) — nothing was run

$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/valid.json5 --contracts tests/cmd/fixtures/nope
? 2
  --contracts tests/cmd/fixtures/nope: tests/cmd/fixtures/nope: no such file or directory
Error: tests/cmd/fixtures/valid.json5: 1 problem(s) — nothing was run

```

— and contracts that do load are counted:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/valid.json5 --contracts ../examples/zk2/tcgui/tc.netif.v1.toml --contracts ../examples/zk2/tcgui/tc.netem.v1.toml
tests/cmd/fixtures/valid.json5: 4 rule(s), 4 sink(s), 2 contract revision(s), doctor every 6h, published as ops-1/zenwatch — ok

```

The daemon is a zk2 service of its own (#614, FL3), at `@hostid.v1/zenwatch`
unless `publish.address` names another; on a host with no id it refuses to
start, naming every way out. An `ephemeral` beside a literal address asks for
two things at once, and is refused at its path:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/publish-ephemeral-literal.json5
? 2
  publish.address: `ephemeral: true` asks hostid.v1 for an ephemeral system, and the address ops-1/zenwatch names its system: write `@hostid.v1/zenwatch`, or drop `ephemeral` (hostid.v1 §2.3)
Error: tests/cmd/fixtures/publish-ephemeral-literal.json5: 1 problem(s) — nothing was run

```

`publish: false` runs no service at all:

```console
$ zenwatch check-config --config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus-run.json5
tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus-run.json5: 1 rule(s), 1 sink(s), no service (publish: false) — ok

```
